  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "A point is chosen uniformly inside a triangle with side lengths 3, 4 and 5. Find the probability that the point chosen is closer to the hypotenuse than to either of the other sides.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{5}{12}$.\n\nThe angle bisectors bound the region closest to the hypotenuse; use its area as a fraction of the triangle’s area.",
      "worked": "The triangle is right-angled. Let $I$ be its incentre and $r$ its inradius. The angle bisectors separate points according to their nearest side, so the required region is the triangle joining $I$ to the two endpoints of the hypotenuse.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac12\\times3\\times4=6\\\\s&=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6\\\\r&=\\frac{A}{s}=\\frac66=1\\\\A_{\\text{required}}&=\\frac12\\times5\\times1=\\frac52\\\\\\Pr(E)&=\\frac{A_{\\text{required}}}{A}=\\frac{5/2}{6}=\\frac5{12}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
